WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.730 --> 00:00:02.730
A Man’s Dreams - Scene Breakdown

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:06.850
In my last video I talked about the deeply
emotional and touching moment of liberation

00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:10.969
that Nico Robin experienced at the top of
the tower of justice on Ennis Lobby.

00:00:10.969 --> 00:00:16.590
And so today I think it’s just appropriate
to continue this elegantly emotional journey,

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:24.259
because -. Oh, sorry I think I just played
the wrong clip there.

00:00:24.259 --> 00:00:30.710
As I was saying, what makes Robin’s character
feel so vulnerable and gentle is the - - Uhm.

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:41.600
So…what I really want to talk to you about
today is how seamlessly and delicately Oda

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:50.309
has designed her - Okay, you know what: Fine,
you’re right.

00:00:50.309 --> 00:00:51.469
Nothing sentimental today.

00:00:51.469 --> 00:00:53.510
Let’s talk Epic and Powerful.

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:58.290
Let’s talk about Blackbeard and why his
infamous speech on Jaya sets him up to be

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:10.220
my favourite villain in One Piece.

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:14.260
One of the things that being stuck in quarantine
made me realise - besides the fact that I

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:19.480
can staple exactly 19 rolls of toilet paper
to the ceiling of my bathroom - is how god

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:24.880
damn excited I am to see Luffy facing of Blackbeard
and his men towards the end of the story.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:30.440
But at the same time, I’m also incredibly
scared what Blackbeard might do to Luffy before

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:35.110
they can face off in battle, like kidnapping
Robin or killing off Shanks.

00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:39.770
And I slowly started going down the rabbit
hole of all the Blackbeard theories out there

00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:42.710
and what he might end up doing in the story.

00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:48.100
And so several YouTube videos, a slightly
too big can of coffee and a short panic attack

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:52.570
where I almost spilled same can of coffee
all across my laptop later, I finally found

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:57.560
myself confronted with the question of all
questions: How the hell do I love Blackbeard

00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:02.220
so much as a villain, if he hasn’t even
properly clashed yet without our hero, expect

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:04.400
for a minor skirmish in Impel Down?

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:10.179
And the answer to this question is exactly
this moment here, in mock town on Jaya at

00:02:10.179 --> 00:02:12.189
the beginning of the Skypea arc.

00:02:12.189 --> 00:02:18.390
It’s an incredibly crafted scene by Oda,
filled to the brim with comedy, epicness and

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:22.959
action, that not only foreshadows a whole
lot of things to come in the story, but also

00:02:22.959 --> 00:02:28.469
contains enormous symbolism for one of the
major themes of One Piece: Dreams.

00:02:28.469 --> 00:02:35.480
So, while this might admittedly be a quite
harsh contrast to Robin’s moment on Ennis

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:41.239
Lobby, I think you will agree with me that
this scene definitely belongs into our ‘The

00:02:41.239 --> 00:02:46.310
iconic scene analysis series’ ‘trademark’
, and so let’s break this moment down and

00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:48.209
see what makes it tick.

00:02:48.209 --> 00:02:52.110
As always I’ll be starting with a short
recap of the moment, that you can but really

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:54.879
shouldn’t skip by going to this timecode
here.

00:02:54.879 --> 00:03:00.590
Also, if you wanna follow along with the manga
, we’ll be discussing chapter 223 ‘till

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:05.040
225 and episode 147 of the anime.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:07.580
Let’s rolllllllll.

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:12.099
The scene takes place shortly after the crew
arrives on Jaya to investigate the way to

00:03:12.099 --> 00:03:16.560
Skypea, of which they have found a map inside
a ship that had fallen from the sky.

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:21.500
After arriving in Mock Town, the main town
of the island, Nami makes Luffy and Zoro promise

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:26.909
to not fight and cause any commotion for them,
while they’re here, already foreshadowing,

00:03:26.909 --> 00:03:30.370
that of course - there is going to be a commotion.

00:03:30.370 --> 00:03:34.950
Shortly after leaving the ship, they encounter
three strange men, each of which will later

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:39.400
turn out to be part of Blackbeard’s crew:
The sniper Van Augur, who is shooting down

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:45.180
birds from a rooftop, the sickly Doc Q, who
is giving out explosive apples to strangers

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:50.390
as well as the wrestler Jesus Burgess, who
is taking out countless people on the street.

00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:54.950
These three, quite unique and important seeming
figures aren’t revealed for who they TRULY

00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:59.579
are until the straw hats leave for Skypea
however, leaving the readers wondering what

00:03:59.579 --> 00:04:02.079
the hell they have just seen.

00:04:02.079 --> 00:04:07.019
While wandering through the city, Luffy, Nami
and Zoro come across a fancy looking hotel

00:04:07.019 --> 00:04:12.290
complex, however immediately after entering
they are told that the establishment is rented

00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:14.650
out by the Bellamy pirates.

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:19.350
Moments later, one of Bellamy’s men appears,
taking them for vagabonds and throwing money

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:20.350
at them.

00:04:20.350 --> 00:04:25.970
And The fact that Nami doesn’t take free
money, I think, speaks for itself here.

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:30.760
After the three straw hats leave, the pirate
finds out about Luffy’s bounty of 30M berry

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:35.480
and shares this information with his captain,
planning to take on Luffy - not knowing that

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:39.860
his bounty has already increased to a 100M.

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:43.900
Meanwhile, Luffy and Co enter one of the local
pubs in order to eat.

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:48.620
There Luffy sits next to a stuffy looking
man and for an entire hilarious sequence of

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:54.010
moments, both Luffy and the man share the
exact identical reactions, except that while

00:04:54.010 --> 00:04:59.750
Luffy hates the food and loves the drinks,
the man feels exactly the opposite.

00:04:59.750 --> 00:05:04.620
They start contending who can order the most
food and in general seem very similar to each

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:05.620
other.

00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:10.760
Luffy is ready to brawl with the man, who
then asks him whether he is a pirate and doesn’t

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:14.730
believe Luffy when he says he has a 30 million
bounty on his head.

00:05:14.730 --> 00:05:18.700
He then leaves the pub, just when Bellamy
and his men arrive.

00:05:18.700 --> 00:05:23.730
Mirroring Shank’s encounter with the mountain
bandit Higuma, the scene begins with a false

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:27.820
sense of good-will, with Bellamy buying Luffy
a drink.

00:05:27.820 --> 00:05:33.850
Before he can take a single zip however, Bellamy
smashes his head into the bar counter.

00:05:33.850 --> 00:05:39.680
Before Bellamy can enjoy his undeniably brave
deed too much though, Zoro already has a blade

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:41.630
right at his throat.

00:05:41.630 --> 00:05:46.710
Luffy also gets back to his feet, preparing
to fight Bellamy and Nami - trying to keep

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:51.890
their goal in mind - asks the barkeeper what
he knows about the sky islands, as they plan

00:05:51.890 --> 00:05:53.060
to journey there.

00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:58.930
At these words Bellamy and all of his men
gathered in the pub break into laughter.

00:05:58.930 --> 00:06:03.720
Switching from laughing to suddenly angry,
Bellamy says that the age where pirates have

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:10.260
dreams is over, mentioning the city of gold,
the emerald capital and even the one piece,

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:13.460
and declares that a new age is coming.

00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:17.360
Looking disgustedly at Luffy he says that
dreams are for losers.

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:23.370
At this moment something visibly changes in
Luffy and when Bellamy and his men start beating

00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:29.190
them up, he tells Zoro not to do anything,
despite Nami’s pleads to fight back now,

00:06:29.190 --> 00:06:33.580
falsely attributing their behaviour to their
earlier promise not to fight.

00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:37.780
After bravely standing up to the pirates’
taunts herself, she finally drags the bloody

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:42.290
Luffy and zoro out of the pub, where in the
middle of the square, the scruffy man from

00:06:42.290 --> 00:06:46.430
earlier is sitting who apparently decided
to witness the brawl.

00:06:46.430 --> 00:06:53.210
He shouts at them that the sky islands are
real and tells Nami that Luffy and Zoro have

00:06:53.210 --> 00:06:55.140
just won this fight.

00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:59.320
In this moment, Luffy remembers Shanks and
Ace and their expectations in him to become

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:00.720
a great pirate.

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:05.390
The man meanwhile continues his epic speech,
saying that the age where pirates can dream

00:07:05.390 --> 00:07:14.550
is not ending, because a man’s dream never
ends.

00:07:14.550 --> 00:07:20.270
While the people around laugh at him, Luffy
and zoro stare back at him without saying

00:07:20.270 --> 00:07:21.480
a word.

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:26.840
He tells Luffy that the road to the top isn’t
easy and that on the way there you come across

00:07:26.840 --> 00:07:32.180
people who aren’t worth your fist and that
he hopes they will make it to the sky islands.

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:37.931
Without a response the straw hats leave and
when Nami wonders who he was, Luffy and Zoro

00:07:37.931 --> 00:07:42.300
respond that it’s probably not him, but
them.

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:47.340
Later on we find out that this man was indeed
Blackbeard and that just like Bellamy, he

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:53.770
now has chosen Luffy as his target, however
for the exact opposite reason.

00:07:53.770 --> 00:07:58.280
Okay so let’s dissect this bad boy of a
scene.

00:07:58.280 --> 00:08:02.330
If you didn’t yet get yourself a cup of
coffee - or tea…*clears throat* - or you

00:08:02.330 --> 00:08:07.460
need a refill, make sure that you’re well
supplied and make yourself comfortable, because

00:08:07.460 --> 00:08:11.640
there is a lot to talk about in this video.

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:16.831
While this scene does a very solid work in
terms of body language, framing, etc., what

00:08:16.831 --> 00:08:22.490
makes this scene truly stand out as an iconic
one piece moment in ‘The iconic scene analysis

00:08:22.490 --> 00:08:27.890
series’ ‘trademark’ , is the enormous
amount of symbolism it carries.

00:08:27.890 --> 00:08:33.110
So in this analysis I will focus heavily on
this aspect and mention other note-worthy

00:08:33.110 --> 00:08:34.749
elements as we go along.

00:08:34.749 --> 00:08:41.089
Okay, *cracking knuckles* so before we dive
into the two heavy themes of dreams and dichotomy

00:08:41.089 --> 00:08:47.820
here, there is one small symbol I wanna talk
about first as a sort of warm-up: Mock Town.

00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:52.980
Oda uses the name of the city here as a symbol
for the story-line itself, as Luffy, Zoro

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:57.410
and Nami are being mocked for their dreams
by Bellamy and his men and Bellamy is in turn

00:08:57.410 --> 00:09:01.319
mocked by Blackbeard for not having a dream
in the first place.

00:09:01.319 --> 00:09:06.430
Oda loves to play around with location names
like this, where for instance Louge town refers

00:09:06.430 --> 00:09:12.050
to the words prologue and epilogue, as Roger’s
journey started and ended here and it also

00:09:12.050 --> 00:09:16.410
marks the endpoint of the straw hats prologue
in the Eastblue before entering the Grand

00:09:16.410 --> 00:09:17.410
Line.

00:09:17.410 --> 00:09:22.220
And - careful very smooth transition incoming
- of course Luffy enters the Grand Line to

00:09:22.220 --> 00:09:27.970
fulfil his Dream of becoming the freest man
in the world and find the One Piece - nailed

00:09:27.970 --> 00:09:28.970
it!

00:09:28.970 --> 00:09:34.290
Okay, let’s talk about a man’s dreams
- or a woman’s dreams I guess.

00:09:34.290 --> 00:09:39.110
Actually, small fun fact, when you read the
Japanese original, Blackbeard simply says

00:09:39.110 --> 00:09:42.130
人の夢は終わらねエ！

00:09:42.130 --> 00:09:45.680
Literally meaning ‘a person’s dream never
ends!’

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:50.360
So depending on the translation you’ve read
I might just blown your mind.

00:09:50.360 --> 00:09:51.360
Or not.

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:52.360
Moving on.

00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:58.240
I hope we’re all on the same page when I
say that dreams are one of, if not THE biggest

00:09:58.240 --> 00:09:59.449
theme in One Piece.

00:09:59.449 --> 00:10:03.910
It for sure is the theme of Luffy and his
crew and it also seems to play an important

00:10:03.910 --> 00:10:08.880
role for the members of the D. Family, a topic
that deserves an entire separate video on

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:10.480
its own.

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:15.980
Dreams are the underlying driver of this entire
scene here on Jaya and play a role in all

00:10:15.980 --> 00:10:18.060
the major events surrounding it.

00:10:18.060 --> 00:10:24.800
Let’s start with Luffy’s encounter with
Bellamy and his men first.

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:31.069
The fundamental question in this moment is
- why do Luffy and Zoro not fight back here?

00:10:31.069 --> 00:10:36.230
The easiest answer here would of course be
that they promised Nami not to cause a commotion

00:10:36.230 --> 00:10:38.949
and they simply want to stay true to their
promise.

00:10:38.949 --> 00:10:43.089
However, Luffy was clearly prepared to brawl
with Blackbeard over nothing more than food

00:10:43.089 --> 00:10:48.579
earlier and if we look at this panel here,
after Luffy gets back up, he also clearly

00:10:48.579 --> 00:10:56.639
is ready to fight Bellamy, just as Zoro is
ready - having his sword at Bellamy’s throat.

00:10:56.639 --> 00:11:01.019
However after he and his men laugh at Nami
for asking about the Sky islands and claiming

00:11:01.019 --> 00:11:04.889
that the era where pirates can dream is over
and that fantasies like the golden city, the

00:11:04.889 --> 00:11:09.880
emerald capital or the One piece are not worth
pursuing, Luffy suddenly seams to loose interest

00:11:09.880 --> 00:11:11.279
in him.

00:11:11.279 --> 00:11:15.920
Why then was Luffy ready to fight Blackbeard
but not Bellamy?

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:20.800
Now you might be tapping your fingers thinking:
“Ohara my man, obviously Luffy didn’t

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:24.990
fight him, because Shanks didn’t fight the
bandits either back in Fusha village!”

00:11:24.990 --> 00:11:30.059
And, I think you’re definitely right in
that this moment with Shanks plays an important

00:11:30.059 --> 00:11:31.249
role here.

00:11:31.249 --> 00:11:34.079
However, I think there is a lot more to it.

00:11:34.079 --> 00:11:39.839
The reason Shanks didn’t fight back against
Higuma and his men was his pacifistic philosophy,

00:11:39.839 --> 00:11:44.499
that basically goes something like this: “If
there is no need to fight and no imminent

00:11:44.499 --> 00:11:49.259
danger to my life or that of my friends, there
is no need for needless violence.”

00:11:49.259 --> 00:11:54.839
Shanks was obviously lightyears out of Higuma’s
reach and as long as he only tried to humiliate

00:11:54.839 --> 00:11:59.639
him, there was no real need to respond to
this and lower yourself to their level.

00:11:59.639 --> 00:12:02.089
The same goes for Luffy in this situation.

00:12:02.089 --> 00:12:06.661
He knows that he is stronger than Bellamy,
which he proves later on in the story, and

00:12:06.661 --> 00:12:11.350
even though he and Zoro actually get physically
harmed, Bellamy wasn't standing in the way

00:12:11.350 --> 00:12:17.819
of Luffy's goals or had hurt any of his friends,
at least yet, and so there was no real need

00:12:17.819 --> 00:12:19.689
to fight back.

00:12:19.689 --> 00:12:24.839
Only when Bellamy beats up Mont Blanc Noland
and takes away his gold, does Luffy come back

00:12:24.839 --> 00:12:30.300
and beat Bellamy with one single punch, just
as Shanks took out all of the bandits once

00:12:30.300 --> 00:12:33.860
they laid hands on Luffy.

00:12:33.860 --> 00:12:39.259
Luffy wants to become a great pirate like
Shanks and Ace and so reacting in the same

00:12:39.259 --> 00:12:43.970
way as his mentor shows the character development
he has gone through since that moment in the

00:12:43.970 --> 00:12:49.399
bar, where kid Luffy was furious for Shanks’
behaviour - which I think is also shows the

00:12:49.399 --> 00:12:51.750
confidence he has in his own powers.

00:12:51.750 --> 00:12:58.010
So yes, the reference to Shanks is obviously
very much intentional here and there is a

00:12:58.010 --> 00:13:03.130
reason that Luffy sees both him and Ace before
his eyes during Blackbeard’s speech and

00:13:03.130 --> 00:13:07.870
we’ll actually get back to this later on
as this might have a second meaning as well.

00:13:07.870 --> 00:13:12.850
However, if this attitude was the only reason
Luffy didn’t fight, why the hell did he

00:13:12.850 --> 00:13:15.930
raise his fists in the first place?

00:13:15.930 --> 00:13:20.529
I think that there is another criterium after
which Luffy decides whether or not someone

00:13:20.529 --> 00:13:24.079
is worth fighting, next to whether they are
a threat or not.

00:13:24.079 --> 00:13:29.160
After all there are a number of fights that
Luffy seems to truly enjoy and cherish.

00:13:29.160 --> 00:13:32.610
And this criterium is: A dream.

00:13:32.610 --> 00:13:35.619
Does the person standing in front of him have
a dream?

00:13:35.619 --> 00:13:38.160
A will to fight for what he believes in?

00:13:38.160 --> 00:13:42.480
And is he striving to fulfil this dream no
matter what?

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:47.529
Blackbeard clearly does have a dream and so
Luffy is more than willing to brawl with him

00:13:47.529 --> 00:13:49.069
and takes him for full.

00:13:49.069 --> 00:13:53.930
The same goes for Burgess, who challenges
everyone in the streets a little earlier,

00:13:53.930 --> 00:13:56.019
stating that he’s the champion.

00:13:56.019 --> 00:13:58.779
Luffy and zoro want to fight him right away.

00:13:58.779 --> 00:14:03.709
The Blackbeard pirates, just like the straw
hats all have their own dream and will they

00:14:03.709 --> 00:14:09.149
follow, no matter how much they might be polar
to each other in terms of values and morality.

00:14:09.149 --> 00:14:13.100
What Bellamy does when laughing at their wish
to find the Sky Island and spitting on the

00:14:13.100 --> 00:14:19.309
era of pirates dreaming, is a clear sign for
Luffy that this man has no dream and thus

00:14:19.309 --> 00:14:20.709
no will of his own.

00:14:20.709 --> 00:14:26.410
Thus he is not on the same level as him and
since he is no immediate threat, he is not

00:14:26.410 --> 00:14:28.100
worth fighting.

00:14:28.100 --> 00:14:33.129
As Zoro confirms, when asked by Nami about
this later, Luffy felt nothing but pity for

00:14:33.129 --> 00:14:38.899
Bellamy in this moment, who is used and disregarded
by the man he admires.

00:14:38.899 --> 00:14:45.779
Doflamingo being a celestial dragon and thus
the arch-nemesis of those that have dreams,

00:14:45.779 --> 00:14:51.470
is wonderfully set up in this moment by Oda,
and the pity that Luffy feels for the dream

00:14:51.470 --> 00:14:56.760
and will-less Bellamy is something that he
beautifully references again on Dressrosa,

00:14:56.760 --> 00:15:02.309
when Luffy calls Bellamy his friend and ultimately
wants to free him from Doflamingo’s control.

00:15:02.309 --> 00:15:07.000
And so, the only reason Luffy finally goes
back to fight Bellamy is because he knows

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:12.589
that the gold city is Mont Blanc’s dream,
and by stealing his gold, the only proof that

00:15:12.589 --> 00:15:18.069
held the old man’s dream together, Bellamy
had mocked this dream as well and taken it

00:15:18.069 --> 00:15:19.069
away.

00:15:19.069 --> 00:15:23.149
Luffy might be naive and and a bit slow on
the uptake sometimes, but his sense for what

00:15:23.149 --> 00:15:26.079
is pure is incredible.

00:15:26.079 --> 00:15:30.550
He believes in friendship, dreams, and dying
for what you believe in.

00:15:30.550 --> 00:15:35.170
This is just one way Oda illustrates that
for us, but I think it’s really hard to

00:15:35.170 --> 00:15:39.119
see that if you don’t believe in dreams
yourself.

00:15:39.119 --> 00:15:42.490
The entire theme of dreams then goes full
circle.

00:15:42.490 --> 00:15:47.009
Before leaving for Skypea and after knocking
out Bellamy, one of his men asks Luffy where

00:15:47.009 --> 00:15:56.949
he is going, to which Luffy replies “Showing
that nothing can stop him from following his

00:15:56.949 --> 00:15:57.949
dreams.

00:15:57.949 --> 00:16:02.949
Finally, after defeating Enel, Luffy rings
the golden bell of Sandora and his Shadow

00:16:02.949 --> 00:16:05.610
can be seen in the sky by Noland and his men.

00:16:05.610 --> 00:16:11.259
Thus, Luffy has confirmed that the golden
city actually exists, legitimising his dream

00:16:11.259 --> 00:16:19.009
and opposing Bellamy’s initial mocking of
both their dreams.

00:16:19.009 --> 00:16:25.610
Okay, let’s talk about Blackbeard.

00:16:25.610 --> 00:16:29.809
As you might have realised, Blackbeard directly
references Roger’s last words here with

00:16:29.809 --> 00:16:31.559
his speech.

00:16:31.559 --> 00:16:35.949
Whether or not he was actually present at
Roger’s funeral, what Oda pretty much foreshadows

00:16:35.949 --> 00:16:41.851
with this parallel here, is that Blackbeard
is also a D. Just like Luffy and Roger, he

00:16:41.851 --> 00:16:46.519
his driven by his dream, which involves finding
the One piece.

00:16:46.519 --> 00:16:51.850
Of course, whatever his dream might be, it
is most likely a lot more self-centred than

00:16:51.850 --> 00:16:57.170
Luffy’s, but it is without any doubt true
that both characters share an insane amount

00:16:57.170 --> 00:16:58.730
of similarities.

00:16:58.730 --> 00:17:05.250
And so, this is the second big theme of this
scene: Dichotomy - or to be more precise the

00:17:05.250 --> 00:17:07.569
dichotomy between Blackbeard and Luffy.

00:17:07.569 --> 00:17:14.510
Let’s look at their first encounter in the
bar.

00:17:14.510 --> 00:17:19.649
Both of them are eating, one loves the pie
and hates the lemonade whereas the other thinks

00:17:19.649 --> 00:17:20.649
the contrary.

00:17:20.649 --> 00:17:26.630
It shows just how perfectly opposed they are
- they think the same way but have perfectly

00:17:26.630 --> 00:17:30.210
opposed point of view on what’s good and
what’s bad.

00:17:30.210 --> 00:17:35.610
The framing wonderfully supports this impression
as both are shown side to side for several

00:17:35.610 --> 00:17:41.330
panels and it seems very deliberate by Oda,
who’s basically telling us “yeah these

00:17:41.330 --> 00:17:43.830
guys are gonna be rivals”.

00:17:43.830 --> 00:17:49.600
Blackbeard is, what writers call a foil - a
character who shares many of the same traits

00:17:49.600 --> 00:17:54.920
and characteristics as the main character,
but differs in one critical way, and is usually

00:17:54.920 --> 00:17:56.640
set against him.

00:17:56.640 --> 00:18:02.450
Luffy and Teach are similar in many core ways,
and started their journey in similar fashion.

00:18:02.450 --> 00:18:07.360
Coming from humble beginnings, the story builds
these characters up over time, showing their

00:18:07.360 --> 00:18:12.940
struggles, their limits, and how they overcome
these obstacles to grow to greater heights.

00:18:12.940 --> 00:18:18.470
They both understand the need for teamwork
and have a very simple, goal-driven mindset.

00:18:18.470 --> 00:18:23.500
However, whereas Luffy gains strength and
infamy through courage, justice and honest

00:18:23.500 --> 00:18:28.250
ambition, Teach advances through deception,
murder and the exploitation of the weakness

00:18:28.250 --> 00:18:30.149
of others.

00:18:30.149 --> 00:18:34.440
Despite their shared attitude that dreams
can never be given up on, the dichotomy of

00:18:34.440 --> 00:18:37.370
their methods resonates with this story arc.

00:18:37.370 --> 00:18:42.100
And so, bringing the conversation back to
the theme of dreams, you could say that Blackbeard

00:18:42.100 --> 00:18:47.200
chose the 'easy' way to achieve his dreams,
since he did what was necessary, but not what

00:18:47.200 --> 00:18:48.980
was right.

00:18:48.980 --> 00:18:53.799
Luffy in contrast will achieve his dreams
WITH his crew mates and most likely they are

00:18:53.799 --> 00:18:57.110
even an active part of his dream in the first
place.

00:18:57.110 --> 00:19:01.730
And I think it’s almost poetic that Blackbeard
doesn’t actually sleep.

00:19:01.730 --> 00:19:06.280
In other words, he doesn’t even have real
dreams to begin with.

00:19:06.280 --> 00:19:13.090
This is so - The way Oda introduces Teach
in this scene by the way might be my favourite

00:19:13.090 --> 00:19:16.409
introduction of any villain ever.

00:19:16.409 --> 00:19:20.539
First of all I think for all the issues I
have had with the One Piece anime over the

00:19:20.539 --> 00:19:25.320
years, the casting of Blackbeard’s Voice
Actor, might have been the best thing they

00:19:25.320 --> 00:19:29.860
every came up with, Akio Ōtsuka.

00:19:29.860 --> 00:19:36.299
That man simply has the perfect range of voice
for this character and I love every line he

00:19:36.299 --> 00:19:42.130
says.

00:19:42.130 --> 00:19:46.860
And the way that the iconic panel itself simply
blows out the entire background and noise

00:19:46.860 --> 00:19:52.590
around them, sousing solely on Blackbeard
opposing the three straw hats is wonderful

00:19:52.590 --> 00:19:58.000
visual foreshadowing for the future conflict
between the two, but also the similarity between

00:19:58.000 --> 00:19:59.000
them.

00:19:59.000 --> 00:20:04.140
And speaking of foreshadowing, the way Luffy
sees Ace and Shanks before his eyes, makes

00:20:04.140 --> 00:20:07.789
me wonder whether this is some sort of foreshadowing
as well.

00:20:07.789 --> 00:20:12.610
Ace has already been taken out by Blackbeard,
and Shanks also has a special relationship

00:20:12.610 --> 00:20:13.820
with him.

00:20:13.820 --> 00:20:15.730
Some food for thought.

00:20:15.730 --> 00:20:21.419
What is also interesting is how Luffy’s
and also Zoro’s attitude changes towards

00:20:21.419 --> 00:20:26.350
Blackbeard and how their words to Nami have
given rise to a number of wild speculations

00:20:26.350 --> 00:20:27.740
about Blackbeard’s powers.

00:20:27.740 --> 00:20:33.250
I don’t wanna dev into these too much, but
simply from an analysis perspective I think

00:20:33.250 --> 00:20:38.580
it becomes clear that Luffy sees another person
siting in the square than who he saw in the

00:20:38.580 --> 00:20:39.690
pub.

00:20:39.690 --> 00:20:44.190
This Blackbeard here is not the Blackbeard
that sat next to him eating pie.

00:20:44.190 --> 00:20:49.210
And so Luffy just stares at him, making clear
that he can’t be fooled.

00:20:49.210 --> 00:20:53.070
This reaction might also ultimately have been
the reason that Blackbeard decided to take

00:20:53.070 --> 00:20:59.320
Luffy out, fearing that he might become his
greatest rival chasing the One Piece.

00:20:59.320 --> 00:21:04.620
Okay, so what is the overall purpose of this
scene?

00:21:04.620 --> 00:21:07.010
As mentioned at the beginning of this video,
yes that was like 4 hours ago, this scene

00:21:07.010 --> 00:21:09.289
is packed to the brim with symbolism.

00:21:09.289 --> 00:21:14.409
The scene works strongly with the overarching
theme of dreams, that is also reflected in

00:21:14.409 --> 00:21:17.970
the introduction of Luffy’s main antagonist
in the story.

00:21:17.970 --> 00:21:23.799
The way Oda introduces Blackbeard is refreshingly
creative, since our first impressions of Blackbeard

00:21:23.799 --> 00:21:28.539
come to us from the stories we hear about
him prior to his official introduction.

00:21:28.539 --> 00:21:33.620
We know that he scared Wapol badly enough
to cause him to abandon Drum Island and that

00:21:33.620 --> 00:21:36.419
Ace is hunting him because he killed a fellow
shipmate.

00:21:36.419 --> 00:21:37.419
Okay.

00:21:37.419 --> 00:21:42.279
It shouldn’t be too hard to identify this
ruthless pirate, right?

00:21:42.279 --> 00:21:46.850
When we are first introduced to the man we
would later come to know as Blackbeard, the

00:21:46.850 --> 00:21:51.399
first thing he does is get into a fight with
Luffy about whether the cherry pie tastes

00:21:51.399 --> 00:21:52.960
good or bad.

00:21:52.960 --> 00:21:58.010
This guy feels important to us, but certainly
not in a bad way.

00:21:58.010 --> 00:22:02.779
Only at the end of the scene do we learn the
truth about who we just met.

00:22:02.779 --> 00:22:07.840
This is a truly fantastic moment in the story
that without any doubt deserves a spot in

00:22:07.840 --> 00:22:09.860
our list.

00:22:09.860 --> 00:22:13.929
While Luffy and Blackbeard got a whole lot
of character development in this scene, who

00:22:13.929 --> 00:22:18.580
also deserves some praise here I feel was
Zoro, who stood next to his captain and took

00:22:18.580 --> 00:22:21.080
the beating without any complaint.

00:22:21.080 --> 00:22:24.510
How did Zoro become such a loyal partner for
Luffy?

00:22:24.510 --> 00:22:27.679
We will be looking at that in the next video.

00:22:27.679 --> 00:22:28.679
Thanks guys.

00:22:28.679 --> 00:22:29.179
Peace.

